## Application service

The FastAPI application serves `/health/live` and `/health/ready` through the
runtime's public `create_app` factory. Interactive docs are off. There is no
business logic, frontend, database schema, migration or seed data.
`kind=fullstack` still generates only the API.

The runtime Git dependency is pinned by `rev` to the explicitly reviewed
`kit_commit`: `{{ kit_commit }}`. That commit must be published and contain the
runtime before a fresh consumer can resolve it. A synthetic SHA used in offline
tests is not a usable release. APM separately retains its reviewed profile tag.

### Configuration

`src/app/settings.py` alone reads environment values: process environment,
then `.env`, then runtime defaults. Copy `.env.example` to the git-ignored `.env`.
Stage-specific examples are `.env.dev.example`, `.env.test.example`,
`.env.staging.example` and `.env.prod.example`; each sets `APP_ENVIRONMENT`
explicitly. For example, `cp .env.dev.example .env` (PowerShell:
`Copy-Item .env.dev.example .env`). Select one file, fill its required fields,
and run `task start`; files are not automatically merged or selected by stage.
Keep only secret-free examples in Git: `.env` and working `.env.*` files are ignored.
Required feature configuration fails at startup without echoing credentials.
Errors name declared fields and the configuration exception class, without
retaining exception context. When a runtime factory does not identify a single
field, the diagnostic names its relevant settings group for review.
Compose 2.24+ loads this optional file; the Docker image never contains it.
Keep secrets in local environment/secret stores, never Copier answers.
Use `.env.example` for local Compose. The other examples keep remote database TLS
enabled and leave endpoints/identities blank until configured. Compose remains
local-only; staging/prod use deployment-injected settings, not the Compose
overrides. Choosing `APP_ENVIRONMENT` labels the application; it does not provision
infrastructure, configure identities, or select a cloud deployment.

Dependencies and build metadata live in `pyproject.toml`; tool configuration
stays in `ruff.toml` and `pytest.ini`. Optional runtime extras are `auth`, `ai`,
`telemetry`, `database`, `retrieval`, `ocr` and `ratelimit`.
The selected executable options already add their extras and wiring. For other
features use `platform-kit-runtime[<extra>]` and explicitly compose their public
APIs; installing an extra alone does not enable behavior. `APP_TRACING=true`
needs the `telemetry` extra and configured `OTEL_*` variables to export spans.
{%- if telemetry == 'application-insights' %}

This project explicitly selected Application Insights. The Azure deployment
supplies `APPLICATIONINSIGHTS_CONNECTION_STRING` and `AZURE_CLIENT_ID`;
ingestion authenticates as that runtime managed identity through
`ManagedIdentityCredential`. Only traces are exported: console collection owns
logs, and no second FastAPI instrumentor or SDK log/metric exporter is enabled.
Exported server spans keep the `http.route` template but drop the raw
`url.path` and `url.query` attributes.
Leave the connection string unset locally; Azure telemetry and credentials are
loaded only for configured export. Verify actual spans and delivery separately.
{%- else %}

Application Insights is not selected. No Azure telemetry dependency or exporter
is needed for local or non-Azure execution. Cloud console collection and
platform alerts remain independent of tracing.
{%- endif %}

The application selects the runtime's opt-in stdlib JSON console logging (INFO
for the application, runtime and Uvicorn; WARNING for other libraries)
and request-context filtering. Access records contain only
method, route template, status, duration and correlation IDs. A handled failure
also logs one `runtime.problem` record with `problem_code`, `instance` and
`failure_reason`, next to its access record, so count requests from
`runtime.http_request`. Raw URLs,
headers, bodies, arbitrary messages and exception text are not serialized.
Errors retain logger/module/function/line, exception class and up to 16 traceback
frame identifiers. Frames contain module/function/line only, never filenames,
source code or locals. Configuration errors also carry field names and cause
class in structured records; ordinary application/Uvicorn errors retain their
code location instead of collapsing into an indistinguishable message.
Health access events remain DEBUG. Uvicorn's raw access logging is disabled.

### Run locally

For the default API, no Azure account, CLI login, cloud resource or APM install
is needed:

```sh
cp .env.example .env
task local:setup
task start
```

PowerShell: `Copy-Item .env.example .env`. Do not overwrite an existing `.env`;
review its values instead. Fill required selected-service settings before startup.

`local:setup` resolves, audits and installs the uv environment only. Review and
commit `uv.lock`; subsequent starts use it without syncing. `task start` runs
`uvicorn app.main:app` on loopback port 8080 with reload and `--no-access-log`.
To debug, run the VS Code **API** launch configuration; it uses the same command.
Dependency downloads and the published toolkit commit are prerequisites, not
steps proven by offline generation tests. Local startup may be quick once
those prerequisites are ready; this is not a measured five-minute installer.

Selected executable options:

- Database: `{{ data }}`.
- Shared rate limiting: `{{ cache }}`.
- Bearer authentication: `{{ auth }}`.
- Internal text generation: `{{ ai_service }}`.
- Azure tracing: `{{ telemetry }}`.
{% if data == 'postgresql' %}

PostgreSQL requires the `APP_POSTGRES_*` settings. Start its local container with
`docker compose --profile postgres up -d postgres`, then `task start`.
For a containerized app include that same profile in `docker compose up --build`.
Compose gives the app container `postgres:5432` and publishes the server on
loopback port `APP_POSTGRES_PORT`; change it if a native PostgreSQL holds 5432.
Host-process settings use loopback.
Keep TLS enabled remotely; `.env.example` disables it only for local development.
The app owns the database lifespan, exposes `app.state.database`, and registers
its actual database probe on `/health/ready`. Consumers can use
`database.session` as a FastAPI dependency or `database.sessions` with the
runtime's `transaction` context. Shutdown disposes the pool.

Local password authentication remains the default and does not import the Azure
identity SDK. For a deployed user-assigned managed identity, explicitly set
`APP_POSTGRES_ENTRA_AUTHENTICATION=true`,
`APP_POSTGRES_MANAGED_IDENTITY_CLIENT_ID` to its client UUID and
`APP_POSTGRES_REQUIRE_TLS=true`. Remove `APP_POSTGRES_PASSWORD` entirely, including
an empty value. A client ID without Entra mode, a missing/invalid client ID,
a supplied password or disabled TLS in Entra mode stops startup safely.
The app constructs `azure.identity.aio.ManagedIdentityCredential` with that
explicit client ID and closes it after database shutdown, including lifespan
startup failure. It never uses `DefaultAzureCredential` or ambient CLI login.
The runtime `database` extra already includes `azure-identity`.
The host identity assignment, PostgreSQL Entra configuration and database-role
mapping to `APP_POSTGRES_USER` remain operator prerequisites; local Compose
PostgreSQL is password-only and does not emulate Entra authentication.
{% endif %}
{% if cache == 'redis' %}

Redis requires both `APP_REDIS_URL` and an explicit JSON `APP_RATE_LIMIT` policy.
Start `docker compose --profile redis up -d redis`, then `task start`.
For a containerized app include that profile in `docker compose up --build`.
Compose uses the internal hostname; host-process settings use loopback.
Remote Redis requires TLS and private access. The app supplies the runtime's
`RedisRateLimitStore` with bounded timeouts and closes its client on shutdown.
Replicas share counters in the project namespace. Runtime rate limiting
**fails open** on Redis failure: this is not an authorization or billing control.
Redis has no generated readiness probe.
{% endif %}
{% if auth == 'entra-bearer' %}

Bearer authentication requires an approved Entra registration and all
`APP_AUTH_*` trust settings. `/technical/identity` requires a validated bearer
token and every configured scope/role, returning only verified tenant/subject
identifiers. The public `open_entra_bearer` factory owns validator HTTP resources.
There are no browser login/session routes; `APP_AUTH_ORIGIN` is required by the
shared runtime configuration but is not a callback registration in this API.
Health remains public. Live token validation needs Entra discovery/JWKS access;
offline tests substitute the validator and do not certify an Entra tenant.
{% endif %}
{% if ai_service == 'text' %}

Local text generation requires explicit `APP_AI_MODEL` and `APP_AI_API_KEY`
settings, plus provider-specific endpoint/version where applicable. Cloud
provisioning instead supplies the OpenAI v1 endpoint, deployment model and
user-assigned Entra identity; no API key is accepted for the provisioned service.
The lifespan creates
the runtime's `TextGenerator` on `app.state.text_generator` and removes that
reference on shutdown. Its public API has no close method or owned client
lifespan. Consumers inject that value into their application service:

```python
generator: TextGenerator = request.app.state.text_generator
result = await generator.generate(request=text_request)
```

Import `TextGenerator` and `TextRequest` from `platform_kit_runtime.ai`; bound
the request's output tokens. No inference route is exposed, and startup makes
no paid model call. Before adding a route, supply authentication, authorization,
budget limits and content handling. Real inference requires separately approved
provider credentials and may incur costs.
{% endif %}

Only opted-in PostgreSQL/Redis services appear in Compose, behind their named
profiles; ports bind to `127.0.0.1`. With both selected, enable both profiles.
No optional service is started automatically by `task start`. For a host port
that is busy on one machine, put a `ports: !override` entry (Compose 2.24.4+) in
the git-ignored `compose.override.yaml`; Compose merges it automatically.

### Test

Start with `task test -- tests/test_health.py -k live` or the test for the
behavior being changed. `task test -- --lf` reruns the last failures;
`task test` runs all application tests. Default discovery is limited to `tests`,
not installed agent capabilities. `task check` fixes formatting/lint and runs
the application suite before integration. `task verify` additionally audits APM
and checks application formatting/lint without changing files; it is not the
per-edit loop.
Tests mock dependency probes; they do not prove live database, Redis, Entra or
model connectivity. Validate real dependencies explicitly in your environment.

### Build the image

```sh
docker build -t {{ project_name }}:local .
```

The locked multi-stage Dockerfile runs as non-root on port 8080. Docker and
network access to published dependencies are needed; rendering proves neither
an image build nor its supply-chain attestation.

### Deployment boundaries

See the [pinned toolkit deployment guide](https://github.com/lfr-ai/agent-kit/blob/{{ kit_commit }}/packages/starter/DEPLOYMENT.md)
for the reviewed deployment contracts and operator prerequisites.

{% if iac == 'none' %}
`iac=none` emits no infrastructure or deployment scaffold. Dockerfile, Compose
and image-publication tooling are still available; none provisions Azure.
{% else %}
`iac={{ iac }}` selects infrastructure and deployment templates. Review their
parameters, secret references, permissions and operator prerequisites before use.
Rendering is not deployment or cloud certification.

Cloud parameters are separate for `dev`, `test`, `staging` and `prod`, each with
its exact stage and distinct names/state. Local is Compose only. Run
`task infra:check-environment ENVIRONMENT=dev` after completing caller-owned
configuration; dispatch rejects mismatching stages before building or logging in.
Protect each {{ 'Azure DevOps' if ci == 'azure-devops' else 'GitHub' }} environment and select a reviewed runner with private-network
access when needed.
{%- if registry == 'acr' %}
Images go to each environment's own Azure Container Registry (`registryName` /
`registry_name`, globally unique); the first deployment of an environment is a
registry bootstrap without the app.
{%- else %}
Images are published to GHCR and must be publicly readable; use this for dev or
evaluation only.
{%- endif %}
Cloud app settings always include the provisioner-owned `APP_ENVIRONMENT` equal
to that selected stage; local settings use `local`.

Dev exposes HTTPS through ACA. Test keeps selected data services private.
Staging and prod are production-like: an internal ACA environment, private DNS,
zone redundancy, at least two replicas and locks; both require an email or
existing-action-group alert destination. Dev/test can explicitly opt out.
ACA handles TLS/load balancing;
`ingress.external=true` in that internal environment means VNet reachability.
Supply your own network CIDRs, resource group and regional model configuration.
PostgreSQL is private even in dev and requires the separate Entra administrator
database-principal/grant step in the deployment guide; first-run readiness is not
unattended. Provisioner-owned service settings cannot be overridden through user
environment values or secrets.

Azure Redis provisioning is currently blocked pending a supported Entra token
renewal/shutdown lifecycle. Existing local Redis with `iac=none` is unaffected;
no cloud key/secret fallback is supplied.
{% endif %}
CI, image publication and any cloud deployment require separate repository
setup, reviewed immutable pins and operator approval. Alert destinations are
environment/IaC parameters, never secrets in Copier answers. Cloud setup,
federation, migrations, production secrets and rollback rehearsal have no
promised completion time and are not part of local startup.
{%- endif %}
